 Because the two are equivalent, it is recommended to always set `default` to the actual value you want to pass and avoid using the special `True` case. This makes the code more explicit and predictable.
+
+This special case does not apply to boolean flags, i.e. flags whose `flag_value` is `True` or `False`. For those, `True` is a legitimate value of the option itself, so `default=True` always means *the default value is `True`*. See the *Negative Flags* section below.
 ````
 
+## Negative Flags
+
+A flag does not have to be phrased positively. To get an option that is *off* by default and whose `flag_value` turns the underlying variable off, combine `flag_value=False` with `default=True`:
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option('--without-xyz', 'enable_xyz', flag_value=False, default=True,
+                  show_default=True, help='Disable xyz.')
+    def info(enable_xyz):
+        click.echo(f"enable_xyz={enable_xyz}")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(info, args=['--help'])
+    invoke(info)
+    invoke(info, args=['--without-xyz'])
+```
+
+Because both the `default` and the `flag_value` are booleans here, they are used as-is: not passing `--without-xyz` gives `True`, passing it gives `False`. This lets the decorated function keep a positive variable name and avoid double negatives such as `if not disable_xyz`.
+
+If you also want an explicit way to turn the feature back on, declare the pair of options as an on/off flag instead, and alias the negative name to the secondary option:
+
+```python
+@click.option('--with-xyz/--without-xyz', 'enable_xyz', default=True)
+```
+
 ## Values from Environment Variables
 
 To pass in a value from a specific environment variable use `envvar`.
